---
title: "Application of Advanced Frameworks for Data Imputation in Conflict Databases"
subtitle: "A comparison of the bias introduced by MICE, Dataset Fusion and Listwise Deletion"
author: "James Hunter"
bibliography: ds_jjh_bib.bib
csl: american-political-science-association.csl
abstract: >
 This paper evaluates the application of Multivariate Imputation by Chained Equations (MICE) to address the pervasive issue of missing data within the Global Terrorism Database (GTD). Critical variables, such as the number of perpetrators, exhibit high rates of missingness that are Missing Not At Random (MNAR) due to the clandestine nature of non-state actors and systematic reporting biases. Consequently, this research critically measures the accuracy, predictive validity, and inherent biases introduced by MICE when synthesizing categorical perpetrator data, specifically distinguishing between independent single actors, small cells, and large groups. By evaluating the imputed dataset's performance metrics against available baseline records and utilizing Zero-Inflated Negative Binomial (ZINB) modeling to account for casualty overdispersion, this study quantifies the confounding effects of imputing MNAR fields. The findings expose the limitations and statistical biases inherent in algorithmic data recovery strategies, ultimately establishing a rigorous framework for evaluating the validity of data synthesis in longitudinal counterterrorism research.
papersize: letter

margin-left: 1in
margin-right: 1in
margin-top: 1in
margin-bottom: 1in
cap-location: top

format: 
  pdf:
    toc: true
    toc-depth: 2
    number-sections: true
    linestretch: 2
    
execute:
  echo: false
  warning: false
  message: false
---

