I also assumed that attacks marked as suicide attacks in the GTD involved at least one perpetrator: The number of perpetrators must at least be nonzero in this case. I also restricted the variables used in my imputation model to primary attack type (attacktype1), primary weapon type (weaptype1), primary target (targtype1), and the broader region and country variables. I did not restrict my model to a specific time or country bounds, in the hopes that the resulting model would retain its validity independent of longitudinal or location confounding effects.

## Refining imputation functions for simultaneous imputation

The high degree of missing fields reflects a strategy by the GTD researchers at START to only include data they can establish with absolute certainty [@GTD2007introducing]. As a result, while information on attackers and outcomes might be fairly robust, if the accounts of an attack only refer to "a handful of attackers" or "an unknown assailant," it is likely that the number of perpetrators would be left blank. I found that for the purposes of statistical analysis of outcomes, defining the exact number of actors is not explicitly required to produce accurate conclusions. Rather, establishing broader categories of Individual, Small Group (2-5 attackers) and Large Group (>5) would be sufficient to convert the number of perpetrators into a categorical factor which could be used for imputation and prediction. In this way, the accuracy and specificity of the imputation model is greatly increased, without compromising the field for analysis purposes. This is because many of the other variables in the GTD are themselves categorical, and as such building predictive models that determine the difference in each additional perpetrator on attack type or group name is not necessarily impactful as compared to analzying the differences in tactics of individuals and groups, which behave very differently.

```{r attack_types_by_nperps_cat}
#| cache: true
#| label: fig4
#| fig-cap: "Differences in attack types and number of perpetrators"
#| fig-width: 12
#| fig-height: 4

excluded_types_nperps <- c("Unknown", "Unarmed Assault", "Hijacking",
                            "Hostage Taking (Barricade Incident)", "Hostage Taking (Kidnapping)")

# Helper: pool completed imputations and compute attack type % by nperps_cat
build_attacktype_plot_data <- function(completed_long) {
  completed_long %>%
    left_join(gtd_clean %>% dplyr::select(eventid, attacktype1_txt), by = "eventid") %>%
    filter(!is.na(attacktype1_txt), !attacktype1_txt %in% excluded_types_nperps,
           !is.na(nperps_cat)) %>%
    group_by(.imp, nperps_cat, attacktype1_txt) %>%
    summarise(n = n(), .groups = "drop") %>%
    group_by(.imp, nperps_cat) %>%
    mutate(pct = n / sum(n) * 100) %>%
    group_by(nperps_cat, attacktype1_txt) %>%
    summarise(pct = mean(pct), .groups = "drop") %>%
    mutate(nperps_cat = ordered(nperps_cat,
             levels = c("Lone Wolf", "Small Cell", "Large Group")))
}

# Model B: nperps_cat imputed directly
attacktype_cat <- build_attacktype_plot_data(
  complete(mice_cat_results, action = "long")
) %>% mutate(model = "Model B: Categorical")

# Model A: derive nperps_cat from imputed continuous nperps
attacktype_cont <- complete(mice_cont_results, action = "long") %>%
  mutate(nperps_cat = case_when(
    nperps == 1               ~ "Lone Wolf",
    nperps > 1 & nperps <= 5  ~ "Small Cell",
    nperps > 5                ~ "Large Group"
  )) %>%
  build_attacktype_plot_data() %>%
  mutate(model = "Model A: Continuous")

# Fix attack type order using Model B proportions as reference
level_order <- attacktype_cat %>%
  group_by(attacktype1_txt) %>%
  summarise(pct = mean(pct), .groups = "drop") %>%
  arrange(pct) %>%
  pull(attacktype1_txt)

attacktype_combined <- bind_rows(attacktype_cat, attacktype_cont) %>%
  mutate(
    attacktype1_txt = factor(attacktype1_txt, levels = level_order),
    model = factor(model, levels = c("Model A: Continuous (RF + PMM)",
                                      "Model B: Categorical (POLR)"))
  )

ggplot(attacktype_combined, aes(x = attacktype1_txt, y = pct, fill = nperps_cat)) +
  geom_col(position = "dodge") +
  geom_text(aes(label = sprintf("%.1f%%", pct)),
            position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), hjust = -0.1, size = 2.5) +
  coord_flip() +
  facet_wrap(~model, ncol = 2) +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.18))) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("Lone Wolf"   = "steelblue",
                                "Small Cell"  = "tomato",
                                "Large Group" = "forestgreen")) +
  labs(
    x = NULL,
    y = "Percentage of Attacks (%)",
    title = "Attack Type Distribution by Perpetrator Group Size:\nModel A (Continuous) vs. Model B (Categorical)",
    fill = NULL
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    legend.position = "right",
    plot.title = element_text(hjust = 0)
  )
```

After ensuring that my training and test data contained a sufficient proportion of each level of perpetrators, I conducted a dry run to establish the imputation method for each variable being imputed. For continuous variables, I used predictive mean matching, which produces a predictive model based off a pool of observed values which most closely match the prediction for the missing case. Random selections are drawn from the pool for each missing field per row. This has the advantage of only selecting potentially plausible values, such as only predicting whole numbers when evaluating the number of attackers. '

For variables that have a high degree of impact on the outcome of a record, but also have a high degree of cardinality such as group name, country, and state/province, I applied a Classification and Regression tree (CART) function. CART operates by building decision trees to recursively partition predictors into pools of similar results, and then builds a tree based off the other low-cardinality predictors (year, region, attack type, weapon type) to sort the records. This sidesteps the dimensionality problem exemplified by random forest entirely by using the predictor space to find similar records, then sampling from their observed values. Like PMM, the imputed value is always a real observed group name, never a nonsensical interpolation.

For the ordered categorical factor I produced grouping the number of perpetrators, I examined the use of a Proportional Odds Logistic Regression (POLR) function. POLR is an extension of binary logistic regression for ordered categorical outcomes. Rather than modeling a single binary boundary, it models K-1 cumulative boundaries simultaneously, one for each transition between adjacent levels, weighing the proportional odds of the predictors at each boundary level. This method does come with a hazard as it establishes the same predictive effect as each boundary is crossed. For instance, 80% of suicide attacks in the GTD involve only a single perpetrator, making this a highly impactful predictor, and of the remainder, only half as many large group attacks were suicide attacks as compared to small groups. With the predictor effect of suicide attacks varying strongly across groups. In my evaluation of the number of perpetrators against the GTD, the difference in predictors between categories (see figure 4) resulted in a strong bias and a 17% reduction in predictive accuracy for the individual category, with accuracy improving by group size. This was a counter-intuitive finding given the low predictive power of incremental changes in the predictors on the number of perpetrators. This was confirmed by running a 

For all other variables being imputed, I used a random forest model. Random Forest's suitability as the default imputation method in MICE pipelines stems from its flexibility and robustness as a non-parametric learner. Unlike parametric methods such as POLR or linear regression, Random Forest imposes no assumptions about the underlying distribution or functional form of relationships in the data, instead discovering structure empirically through recursive partitioning. This allows it to capture complex interactions and non-linear relationships between predictors — such as the interplay between region, attack type, and year in determining perpetrator counts that parametric methods would miss or misrepresent. Its native handling of mixed data types eliminates the need for manual preprocessing or dummy coding of categorical variables, and its ensemble aggregation naturally dampens the influence of irrelevant predictors, since uninformative variables are selected at splits only by chance and contribute little to the final predictions. Together these properties make Random Forest a robust, largely assumption-free default that can be applied broadly across the heterogeneous predictor space of a dataset like the GTD without requiring variable-by-variable method tuning.

## Exlusion of predictors and imputed fields

It is also critical to ensure the MICE imputation process avoids using some variables as predictors that would be highly colinear in a regression model (such as the event id) or too closely related to another value, such as including on both country code and country name. Other values such as weapon type were excluded due to a large proportion of dissimilar categories which simultaneously have very few records. My testing revealed multiple weapon types which caused MICE to inflict ridge penalties in the row prediction due to a lack of covariates for the imputation functions to pull records upon, and binning weapon types across dissimilar categories was impractical.

## Computational resources and Performance

My final collection of imputation runs were performed on a personal computer with 8 CPU cores and 32 GB of RAM. Predicting the continuous number of perpetrators value required 8 rounds of 20 iterations each, while predicting the grouped number of perpetrators only required 8 rounds of 10 iterations. The number of rounds leveraged the use of running each round of iterations in parallel to leverage each CPU core (in groups of 4 rounds), improving the runtime from 2 days per imputation run when run serially to roughly 5 hours per model when run in parallel. 

The iteration values were determined by an evaluation of the convergence between rounds, demonstrating MICE's improvement process as the randomly-selected predictor weights from each random forest model reach a consensus by comparison.

```{r mice_convergance_plots, include=TRUE}
#| cache: true
#| label: fig5
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| fig-width: 6
#| fig-height: 3
#| fig-cap: "Convergance Trace Plot comparison of MICE imputation models"
#| fig-align: "center"

# Helper: extract mean and SD per iteration per chain from a mids object
extract_trace <- function(mids_obj, var_name) {
  cm <- mids_obj$chainMean          # array [vars, iterations, chains]
  cv <- mids_obj$chainVar           # array [vars, iterations, chains]
  n_iter   <- dim(cm)[2]
  n_chains <- dim(cm)[3]
  chain_labels <- paste0("Chain ", seq_len(n_chains))

  mean_long <- as.data.frame(cm[var_name, , ]) %>%
    setNames(chain_labels) %>%
    mutate(Iteration = seq_len(n_iter)) %>%
    pivot_longer(-Iteration, names_to = "Chain", values_to = "Value") %>%
    mutate(Metric = "Mean")

  sd_long <- as.data.frame(sqrt(cv[var_name, , ])) %>%
    setNames(chain_labels) %>%
    mutate(Iteration = seq_len(n_iter)) %>%
    pivot_longer(-Iteration, names_to = "Chain", values_to = "Value") %>%
    mutate(Metric = "Std. Deviation")

  bind_rows(mean_long, sd_long)
}

trace_cont <- extract_trace(mice_cont_results, "nperps")
trace_cat  <- extract_trace(mice_cat_results,  "nperps_cat")

# Shared plot function: facets for Mean / SD, one line per chain
plot_trace <- function(trace_df, title) {
  ggplot(trace_df, aes(x = Iteration, y = Value, color = Chain, group = Chain)) +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.5, alpha = 0.85) +
    facet_wrap(~Metric, scales = "free_y", ncol = 1) +
    scale_color_brewer(palette = "Set1") +
    labs(title = title, x = "Iteration", y = NULL, color = NULL) +
    theme_minimal(base_size = 9) +
    theme(
      plot.title    = element_text(hjust = 0.5, face = "bold", size = 9),
      strip.text    = element_text(face = "bold"),
      legend.position = "bottom"
    )
}

p_cont <- plot_trace(trace_cont, "Model A: Continuous (nperps)")
p_cat  <- plot_trace(trace_cat,  "Model B: Categorical (nperps_cat)")

p_cont + p_cat + plot_layout(guides = "collect") & theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

After training, I generated a confusion matrix against the unimputed data for comparison purposes. The results of the MICE model reflect a relatively high degree of accuracy in predicting if an attack was perpetrated by an individual (90.7%), with decreasing performance for the small and large group categories. I then applied logistic regression against my imputed results to examine the differences in outcomes and tactics, if any, between attacks by individuals and groups. I determined that while individuals and groups have similar outcomes when the same attack and weapon types are considered, individual terrorist attackers are far more likely to employ vehicles and to commit suicide attacks. Region and Target Type were not strong predictors on their own, although comparisons within the region category show an increased probability of single actor terrorism in the Levant in comparison to Asia, where terrorist attacks involve more actors working in groups. Radiological weapons were among a handful of weapon types that needed to be excluded from the logistic regression visualization due to quasi-complete separation caused by insufficient event frequency.

```{r mice_accuracy, include=FALSE}
#| cache: true

# --- Evaluation design ---
# test_ids / train_ids are defined in mice_nperps (stratified by nperps_cat).
# Evaluation datasets mask test-row nperps/nperps_cat to NA while leaving
# training rows and originally-missing rows untouched. This faithfully simulates
# production: MICE only has access to training-row observations when imputing
# test rows — no leakage from test-row true values.
# m and maxit match the production run (10 / 20) so metrics reflect actual
# model quality. Predictions are pooled across all 5 imputations before
# evaluation rather than drawn from a single imputed dataset.

# Helper: modal value across a character vector (for ordered factor pooling)
modal_val <- function(x) {
  tbl <- table(x)
  names(tbl)[which.max(tbl)]
}

# Ground truth for test rows, sorted by eventid for deterministic alignment
truth_lookup <- gtd_clean %>%
  filter(eventid %in% test_ids) %>%
  dplyr::select(eventid, nperps_cat) %>%
  arrange(eventid)

true_categories <- factor(truth_lookup$nperps_cat,
                          levels = c("Lone Wolf", "Small Cell", "Large Group"))

# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODEL A: CONTINUOUS IMPUTATION EVALUATION
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
eval_data_cont <- gtd_clean %>%
  dplyr::select(all_of(base_vars), nperps) %>%
  mutate(across(all_of(factor_vars), as.factor)) %>%
  # Mask test rows only — training-row values remain visible to MICE
  mutate(nperps = if_else(eventid %in% test_ids, NA_real_, nperps))

progressr::handlers("txtprogressbar")
with_progress({
  eval_imp_cont <- futuremice(eval_data_cont,
                              m = 8,
                              maxit = 20,
                              method = imp_meth_cont,
                              predictorMatrix = pred_mat_cont,
                              parallelseed = 101,
                              n.core = 4)
})

# Average nperps across all 5 imputations per test row, then bin.
# Averaging before binning is more stable than voting on bin labels directly
# for a continuous variable.
imputed_cat_from_cont <- bind_rows(
  lapply(1:8, function(i)
    complete(eval_imp_cont, i) %>%
      filter(eventid %in% test_ids) %>%
      dplyr::select(eventid, nperps)
)) %>%
  group_by(eventid) %>%
  summarise(nperps_avg = mean(nperps), .groups = "drop") %>%
  arrange(eventid) %>%
  mutate(
    pred_cat = case_when(
      round(nperps_avg) == 1                             ~ "Lone Wolf",
      round(nperps_avg) > 1 & round(nperps_avg) <= 5    ~ "Small Cell",
      round(nperps_avg) > 5                              ~ "Large Group",
      TRUE                                               ~ NA_character_
    )
  ) %>%
  pull(pred_cat) %>%
  factor(levels = c("Lone Wolf", "Small Cell", "Large Group"))

# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODEL B: CATEGORICAL IMPUTATION EVALUATION
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
eval_data_cat <- gtd_clean %>%
  dplyr::select(all_of(base_vars), nperps_cat) %>%
  mutate(across(all_of(factor_vars), as.factor)) %>%
  # Mask test rows only
  mutate(nperps_cat = if_else(eventid %in% test_ids,
                              NA_character_, as.character(nperps_cat)),
         nperps_cat = ordered(nperps_cat,
                              levels = c("Lone Wolf", "Small Cell", "Large Group")))

progressr::handlers("txtprogressbar")
with_progress({
  eval_imp_cat <- futuremice(eval_data_cat,
                             m = 8,
                             maxit = 10,
                             method = imp_meth_cat,
                             predictorMatrix = pred_mat_cat,
                             post = post_cat,
                             parallelseed = 101,
                             n.core = 4)
})

# Modal vote across all 5 imputations per test row.
# For an ordered factor, majority vote is the appropriate pooling rule.
imputed_cat_from_cat <- bind_rows(
  lapply(1:8, function(i)
    complete(eval_imp_cat, i) %>%
      filter(eventid %in% test_ids) %>%
      dplyr::select(eventid, nperps_cat) %>%
      mutate(nperps_cat = as.character(nperps_cat))
)) %>%
  group_by(eventid) %>%
  summarise(pred_cat = modal_val(nperps_cat), .groups = "drop") %>%
  arrange(eventid) %>%
  pull(pred_cat) %>%
  factor(levels = c("Lone Wolf", "Small Cell", "Large Group"))
```

```{r conf_table}
#| cache: true
#| label: fig6
#| fig-cap: "Performance Metrics of MICE Imputation by Category"
# Generate both Confusion Matrices
conf_matrix_cont <- confusionMatrix(data = imputed_cat_from_cont, reference = true_categories)
conf_matrix_cat <- confusionMatrix(data = imputed_cat_from_cat, reference = true_categories)

# Extract metrics and strip "Class: " prefix from row names
metrics_cont <- as.data.frame(conf_matrix_cont$byClass[, c("Sensitivity", "Specificity")])
metrics_cont$Class <- gsub("Class: ", "", rownames(metrics_cont))

metrics_cat <- as.data.frame(conf_matrix_cat$byClass[, c("Sensitivity", "Specificity")])
metrics_cat$Class <- gsub("Class: ", "", rownames(metrics_cat))

# Pivot wide: one row per class, paired columns per model
metrics_wide <- metrics_cont %>%
  mutate(Class = factor(Class, levels = c("Lone Wolf", "Small Cell", "Large Group"))) %>%
  arrange(Class) %>%
  rename(Cont_Sens = Sensitivity, Cont_Spec = Specificity) %>%
  left_join(
    metrics_cat %>% rename(Cat_Sens = Sensitivity, Cat_Spec = Specificity),
    by = "Class"
  ) %>%
  dplyr::select(Class, Cont_Sens, Cont_Spec, Cat_Sens, Cat_Spec)

# Generate the table
metrics_wide %>%
  kbl(
    caption = "Performance Metrics: Predicting from Continuous Data vs Binned Categories",
    booktabs = TRUE,
    digits = 3,
    align = c("l", "c", "c", "c", "c"),
    col.names = c("", "Sensitivity", "Specificity", "Sensitivity", "Specificity")
  ) %>%
  kable_classic(full_width = FALSE, html_font = "Cambria") %>%
  add_header_above(c(" " = 1, "Continuous Data" = 2, "Binned Categories" = 2))
```

