```{r rootogram_plot_3, include=FALSE}
#| cache: true
plot_limit <- 25

# Use a single completed imputation so rootogram reflects the actual imputed data
# rather than the listwise-deleted gtd_clean_final subset (83% missing nperps)
gtd_imputed_single <- complete(mice_cont_results, 1) %>%
  mutate(nperps_cat = case_when(
    nperps == 1               ~ "Lone Wolf",
    nperps > 1 & nperps <= 5  ~ "Small Cell",
    nperps > 5                ~ "Large Group"
  ),
  nperps_cat = ordered(nperps_cat,
                        levels = c("Lone Wolf", "Small Cell", "Large Group"))) %>%
  filter(!is.na(nkill))

plot_nb <- glm.nb(nkill ~ nperps_cat + region + attacktype1 + weaptype1,
                  data = gtd_imputed_single)

# ZINB: fit on raw gtd_clean_final (83% missing nperps) to demonstrate
# how high missingness undermines reliable model estimation even with ZINB
plot_zinb <- zeroinfl(nkill ~ nperps_cat + region | attacktype1 + weaptype1,
                      data = gtd_clean_final, dist = "negbin")
```

```{r rootogram_plot_4, include=FALSE}
#| cache: true

# NB expected frequencies from imputed data
mu_nb    <- predict(plot_nb, type = "response")
theta_nb <- plot_nb$theta
expected_freq_nb <- sapply(0:plot_limit, function(i)
  sum(dnbinom(i, mu = mu_nb, size = theta_nb)))

# ZINB expected frequencies from raw missing data — manual component decomposition
# avoids broken predict(type = "prob") and correctly applies ZINB probability mass
mu_zinb    <- predict(plot_zinb, type = "count")
pi_zinb    <- predict(plot_zinb, type = "zero")
theta_zinb <- plot_zinb$theta
expected_freq_zinb <- sapply(0:plot_limit, function(i) {
  p_i <- (1 - pi_zinb) * dnbinom(i, mu = mu_zinb, size = theta_zinb) +
         if (i == 0) pi_zinb else 0
  sum(p_i)
})

# Each rootogram uses observed frequencies from its own data source
observed_freq_nb   <- table(factor(gtd_imputed_single$nkill, levels = 0:plot_limit))
observed_freq_zinb <- table(factor(gtd_clean_final$nkill,    levels = 0:plot_limit))

df_nb <- data.frame(
  Count = 0:plot_limit,
  Observed = as.numeric(observed_freq_nb),
  Expected = as.numeric(expected_freq_nb)
) %>%
  mutate(Sqrt_Obs = sqrt(Observed), Sqrt_Exp = sqrt(Expected), Bottom = Sqrt_Exp - Sqrt_Obs)

df_zinb <- data.frame(
  Count = 0:plot_limit,
  Observed = as.numeric(observed_freq_zinb),
  Expected = as.numeric(expected_freq_zinb)
) %>%
  mutate(Sqrt_Obs = sqrt(Observed), Sqrt_Exp = sqrt(Expected), Bottom = Sqrt_Exp - Sqrt_Obs)
```

```{r rootogram_plot_5, include=FALSE}
#| cache: true
# Helper function
build_rootogram <- function(data, title) {
  ggplot(data, aes(x = Count)) +
    geom_segment(aes(x = Count, xend = Count, y = Sqrt_Exp, yend = Bottom), 
                 linewidth = 4, color = "steelblue", alpha = 0.8) +
    geom_line(aes(y = Sqrt_Exp), color = "red", linewidth = 1) +
    geom_point(aes(y = Sqrt_Exp), color = "red", size = 2) +
    geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", color = "black") +
    labs(
      title = title,
      x = "Number of Fatalities (nkill)",
      y = expression(sqrt("Frequency"))
    ) +
    theme_minimal() +
    theme(plot.title = element_text(face = "bold", size = 12))
}
```

```{r rootogram_plot_6, include=TRUE}
#| cache: true
#| label: fig9
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| fig-width: 6
#| fig-height: 3
#| fig-cap: "Rootogram Display of the predicitve over or underfit of the model on the number of casualties from the continuous imputed dataset"
#| fig-align: "center"
# Create both plots
plot1 <- build_rootogram(df_nb, "Standard Negative\nBinomial model")
plot2 <- build_rootogram(df_zinb, "Zero-Inflated\nNegative Binomial")
plot1 | plot2
```

A rootogram of the imputed and un-imputed datasets was generated with a negative binomial model, and compared to a rootogram of the unimputed data. Because the unimputed data has a very high level of missignness, I employed a Zero-Inflated Negative Binomial in an attempt to fit the data while accounting for the missing values. This handles the dispersion of the variables well, and the results indicate that the predictive power between the imputed data with a standard negative binomial model and the unimputed data with a zero-inflated negative binomial exhibit similar (but not identical) characteristics. This leads me to conclude that the model weights used for data imputation reveal trends in the data that expose the MNAR confounding effect. For instance, suicide attacks are significantly more likely to be perpetrated by a single individual, and attacks against abortion clinics and religious figures also lean towards being perpetrated by individuals. If future attacks were seeking attribution or being investigated, the weights here provide a starting reference point.


