forest_plot_quad
```

As MICE is primarily designed for MAR data and the missing fields are assuredly MNAR, I sought to examine if the imputation results would hold under this conditions. A tipping-point analysis against the suicide flag, a highly related predictor of if the number of perpetrators, held up until a delta of 2. This indicates that with careful treatment and preparation of variables, the imputation process holds potential even for highly missing fields.

```{r missingness_exploration, include=FALSE}
#| cache: true
gtd_clean <- gtd_clean %>% mutate(nperps_missing = as.integer(is.na(nperps)))

miss_model <- glm(nperps_missing ~ attacktype1 + weaptype1_cat + targtype1 +
                    region + iyear + suicide + nkill + nwound,
                  data = gtd_clean, family = binomial)
summary(miss_model)

# Pseudo-R² gives a sense of how much observed vars explain missingness
library(pscl)
pR2(miss_model)["McFadden"]

#A McFadden R² above ~0.15 suggests the observed covariates explain a meaningful share of missingness, supporting MAR. Near zero means predictors don't explain why values go missing and MNAR becomes more plausible.

# Visualise missingness rate of nperps across factor levels
gg_miss_fct(gtd_clean %>% dplyr::select(nperps, region, attacktype1, weaptype1_cat, iyear), fct = region)

# Formal MCAR test (expect rejection, but inspect the residuals)
mcar_result <- mcar_test(gtd_clean %>%
  dplyr::select(nperps, nkill, nwound, attacktype1, targtype1, region, suicide))

mcar_result
# This result of 0 formally proves that the data is not MCAR. The below tipping-point test disproves the MAR hypothesis. The only possibility left is NMAR.
```

