    pct_suicide     = mean(suicide == 1, na.rm = TRUE),
    pct_bombing     = mean(attacktype1 == 3, na.rm = TRUE),  # Bombing/explosion
    pct_gname_known = mean(!is.na(gname)),
    n               = n()
  )
```

```{r proxy_variable_auxiliary_correlation, include=FALSE}
#| cache: true
# The START coding protocol omits nperps when counts cannot be confirmed. Attacks that are harder to confirm tend to be smaller, more diffuse, less claimed by a group.

# Construct a "documentation quality" proxy
gtd_clean <- gtd_clean %>%
  mutate(
    doc_quality = case_when(
      !is.na(gname) & !is.na(nkill) & !is.na(nwound) ~ "High",
      !is.na(gname) | (!is.na(nkill) & !is.na(nwound)) ~ "Medium",
      TRUE ~ "Low"
    )
  )

# Test whether documentation quality predicts nperps missingness
#   above and beyond your existing covariates
full_model <- glm(is.na(nperps) ~ attacktype1 + weaptype1_cat + targtype1 +
                    region + iyear + suicide + nkill + nwound + doc_quality,
                  data = gtd_clean, family = binomial)

restricted_model <- glm(is.na(nperps) ~ attacktype1 + weaptype1_cat + targtype1 +
                          region + iyear + suicide + nkill + nwound,
                        data = gtd_clean, family = binomial)

lrtest(restricted_model, full_model)  # from lmtest, already in your setup

# The GTD codebook provides it directly: perpetrator counts are only recorded when they can be confirmed from multiple independent sources. This means:
#Large organised group attacks (bombings by named groups, coordinated assaults) generate claims of responsibility, survivor accounts, security footage, and press coverage: the count gets recorded.
# Lone-wolf and small-cell attacks are frequently committed by individuals who are killed, flee, or are identified only after investigation: at the time of coding, the count is genuinely unknown and is left blank.
# This is a textbook MNAR mechanism: P(nperps is missing) is highest precisely when nperps is small, because small attacks are the hardest to confirm. The missing value predicts its own missingness. Write this out explicitly in your methodology section — it converts a statistical inference into a theoretically grounded claim and is more convincing to a reviewer than any p-value.
```

# Conclusion

This research demonstrates that Multiple Imputation by Chained Equations can recover substantively meaningful perpetrator group classifications from a dataset in which 83% of perpetrator count records are missing, provided the imputation pipeline is carefully designed around the structure of the missingness. Formal testing confirmed that the GTD's perpetrators data is Missing Not At Random: the probability of missingness is highest precisely when the true perpetrator count is smallest, because lone wolf and small cell attacks are the hardest to confirm from independent sources at the time of coding. Despite this unfavorable missingness mechanism, the continuous imputation model (Model A) achieved 90.7% sensitivity in correctly classifying lone wolf attacks after binning imputed perpetrator counts into the three-category scheme, with decreasing but still interpretable performance across the Small Cell and Large Group categories. A tipping-point sensitivity analysis further demonstrated that the key predictor relationships held even when all imputed perpetrator counts were shifted upward by two, the most pessimistic plausible deviation from the MAR assumption. This confirms that the primary conclusions are robust to the NMAR bias that listwise deletion or naive imputation would have introduced.

The logistic regression results pooled across imputed datasets reveal that individual and group attackers differ more in their choice of method than in their ultimate outcomes. When attack type and weapon type are held constant, lone wolf and group attacks produce comparable casualty distributions. However, non-parametric testing of unadjusted casualty counts identified a highly significant distributional difference between lone wolves and multi-perpetrator attacks (H(2) = 1210.5, p < 0.001), with a notable threshold effect: Small Cell and Large Group attacks were statistically indistinguishable from one another (p = 0.53), suggesting that introducing even a minimal network of two to five actors fundamentally shifts the lethality profile of an attack to match that of large organized groups. Tactically, lone wolf attackers are disproportionately likely to employ vehicles and to commit suicide attacks, while region and target type were weak standalone predictors, with the exception of the Levant, which exhibited elevated individual-actor probability relative to Asia where group-based attacks predominate.

These findings carry practical implications for both counterterrorism risk assessment and for the broader methodological literature on imputation in conflict databases. The comparison between the attack type distributions of the full GTD, the listwise deletion subset, and the GTD-ACLED shared event set illustrates how conventional data reduction strategies systematically distort the apparent prevalence of bombing and armed assault relative to other attack types, a bias that compounds when downstream models treat the reduced dataset as representative. By contrast, MICE with method selection tailored to each variable's distribution and cardinality produced a recoverable signal from a field that prior quantitative analyses have largely discarded. Future work should explore whether the emerging ensemble-based meta-imputation frameworks can further reduce the residual NMAR bias, particularly those that incorporate missingness indicator variables directly into the imputation model.

\clearpage

# Appendix 1: Imputation methods against other datasets

In the tangential field of Biomedical data, Azad has shown the promise of ensemble-based methods that combine contextual models with ensemble models to produced balanced approaches that adjust weights of predictors across multiple missing fields [@Azad2025]. This approach may allow analysts to bridge the gap between records where generating a RAG of applicable context (such as English language news articles for models that are training against English language data) is not available.

Accounting for the level of missingness using a mask indicator is also useful to identify patterns in NMAR datasets. Using this method, Perez-Lebel benchmarked imputation approaches on healthcare databases and found that tree-based models utilizing Missing Incorporated in Attributes (MIA) provide the best balance of accuracy and speed [@PerezLebel2022]. When data is weakly correlated, a Ranked k-Nearest Neighbors (RkNN) can perform well. @Khan2024 introduces the RkNN algorithm to refine traditional kNN imputation. By utilizing Ranked Set Sampling, this algorithm better handles data variability and reduces error when variables are weakly correlated or lack sufficient nearby neighbors.

Extensive research has been performed leveraging ensemble frameworks to reduce variance and improve prediction accuracy. @Aleryani2020 proposed Multiple Imputation Ensembles (MIE), a system where multiple imputation techniques and ensemble methods collaborate to produce a superior final prediction when data is Missing Completely At Random. @Azad2025 detailed the Meta-Imputation Balanced (MIB) framework, which trains a supervised meta-model on synthetically masked data to intelligently weigh the outputs of multiple base imputers. These methods are automated, powerful tools, which trade computational performance for accuracy. A key advantage is the ability for the models to seek and identify statistically significant fields on their own, although this can also be a hazard when colinear variables or keys are included for contention, reducing the external validity of the model to impute new data.

The authors above have all successfully used ensemble-based models of various flavors to produce highly accurate predictive models. However, maintaining accuracy and reproducability of the model for new datasets is also critical. Štrumbelj and Kononenko discuss the process of evaluating the impact of ensemble models in the final product by adapting the Shapley value. The authors quantify the "fair" contribution of each feature value to a specific classification outcome, effectively revealing how much each variable influences a model's decision. To resolve the exponential time complexity typically associated with these calculations, the paper proposes a sampling-based approximation method that makes the technique computationally feasible for complex datasets [@JMLR:v11:strumbelj10a].

Another option for field synthesis is the use of additional data sources connected without the direct tie between two fields. @Cai2025 developed a sophisticated AI-driven tool called FAME (Fingerprint to Article Matching for Events) to systematically track how global media outlets report on natural disasters and terrorist attacks. By utilizing a "fingerprint" constructed from a specific combination of event class, location, and date, the system filters millions of multilingual articles to identify those covering specific crises without the need for manual training data. This methodology reveals that news coverage is heavily biased toward wealthier nations and those with significant trade ties, often requiring much higher death tolls in poorer regions to receive equivalent attention. Ultimately, this work provides an open-source framework for computational social science to measure and understand the systemic factors that shape the global news agenda.

